To create an interactive check-in page with popups, you can use your current approach but modify it a bit to ensure the popups behave as expected. Here's a more comprehensive solution with both HTML and CSS enhancements.

### HTML Structure
You should have an element for the emotion and another for the popup content. Here’s a basic structure:

```html
<div class="emotion-container">
    <div class="happy-thumb" onclick="togglePopup(event)">
        <p>Happy</p>
        <span class="popup-content" id="happyPopup">I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Wonderful are your works; my soul knows it very well. Psalm 139:14</span>
    </div>
    <!-- Add more emotions as needed -->
</div>
```

### CSS for Popup
You need to create styles for the popup to be hidden by default and displayed when the "show" class is added.

```css
.popup-content {
    display: none; /* Hidden by default */
    position: absolute; /* Positioned relative to the container */
    background-color: #fff; /* Background color of the popup */
    border: 1px solid #ccc; /* Border for the popup */
    padding: 10px; /* Padding inside the popup */
    z-index: 100; /* Stack on top */
}

.popup-content.show {
    display: block; /* Show the popup when 'show' class is added */
}
```

### JavaScript for Toggle Functionality
Update your JavaScript to toggle visibility correctly. You also want to ensure that if another popup is visible, it gets closed:

```javascript
function togglePopup(event) {
    // First, close any other open popups
    var popups = document.querySelectorAll('.popup-content');
    popups.forEach(function(popup) {
        if (popup !== event.currentTarget.querySelector('.popup-content')) {
            popup.classList.remove('show');
        }
    });

    // Now toggle the clicked popup
    var popup = event.currentTarget.querySelector('.popup-content');
    popup.classList.toggle('show');
}
```

### Explanation of the Code
1. **HTML**: Each emotion section has a `div` that contains both the emotion name and a `span` for the popup text.
2. **CSS**: The popup `span` is initially hidden using `display: none`, and when the `show` class is added, it becomes visible.
3. **JavaScript**: The `togglePopup` function toggles the visibility of the popup on click and ensures that only one popup can be visible at a time.

### Conclusion
With these modifications, your interactive check-in page should function as intended, displaying a corresponding scripture popup based on the emotion selected. Remember to test it thoroughly to ensure smooth user experience.

